#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# find_instance

#% text
# `find_instance(cond, x)` gives one point satisfying `cond` as `[[x -> v]]`; `find_instance(cond, [x, y])` for several variables; `[]` when there is none.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# With one variable the condition is reduced by `reduce` and a root or a simple number inside an interval is taken.

#% item
# With several variables the equations are solved by `solve` and the unknowns left free are set to 0.

#% item
# Real polynomial conditions with an inequality (or the domain `reals`) take a sample point of the cylindrical algebraic decomposition; `find_instance(cond, vars, reals, n)` lists up to `n` points of a finite solution set.

#% item
# `find_instance(cond, vars, integers)` and `find_instance(cond, vars, integers, n)`: Pell equations `x^2 - D y^2 == 1` through the powers of the fundamental unit, bounded problems by bounds from the real decomposition and a complete search of the box, others by a search of the integer points with coordinates up to 60 in absolute value. Every point is checked exactly; when none is found the call stays unevaluated.

#% item
# In one integer variable that no other method takes (number functions such as `prime_pi` or `mod` in the condition),
# the integers 0, 1, -1, 2, -2, ... up to 20000 in absolute value are tried in turn: the first ones at which the
# condition evaluates to `true`.

#% item
# `positive_integers`, `nonnegative_integers`, `negative_integers` and `nonpositive_integers` are the integers with a sign condition on each variable. A sum of squares `x1^2 + ... + xk^2 == N` is decomposed directly, largest square first.

#% item
# Several integer variables with other functions in the condition (`euler_phi`, `power_mod`): the integer points of the box of the polynomial conditions, the whole condition evaluated at each.

#% item
# `find_instance(cond, p, primes)` (or `element(p, primes)` in the condition): the primes from the lower bound of the condition upward.

#% item
# Over the complex numbers (no domain), equations with functions such as `sin`, `exp` or Bessel and Hankel functions: an equation `solve` solves for one unknown gives it in terms of the others, the other unknowns of the last equation are set to 1, 2, 1/2, ..., and the last unknown is a value of `solve` or a proved root object. The equations hold by construction; the other conditions must evaluate to `true` at the point.

#% item
# `find_instance(cond, vars, n)` without a domain lists up to `n` points: all of them for a finite solution set, else points of the solution families (periodic equations with their integer parameter set to 0, 1, -1, ...). Over the reals, sets of positive dimension give sample points of several cells.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# A root:

#% cell
find_instance(x^2 == 4 and x > 0, x)
#% output
#= [[x -> 2]]

#% text
# A point of an interval:

#% cell
find_instance(x^2 < 4 and x > 1, x)
#% output
#= [[x -> 3/2]]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Two equations:

#% cell
find_instance(x + y == 3 and x - y == 1, [x, y])
#% output
#= [[x -> 2, y -> 1]]

#% text
# An underdetermined system:

#% cell
find_instance(x + y == 3, [x, y])
#% output
#= [[x -> 3, y -> 0]]

#% text
# No solution:

#% cell
find_instance(x^2 < 0, x)
#% output
#= []

#% text
# All the points of a finite real set:

#% cell
find_instance(x^2 + y^2 == 1 and x == 2*y + 1, [x, y], reals, 5)
#% output
#= [[x -> -3/5, y -> -4/5], [x -> 1, y -> 0]]

#% text
# A solution of a Pell equation in a range:

#% cell
find_instance(x^2 - 3*y^2 == 1 and 10 < x and x < 100, [x, y], integers)
#% output
#= [[x -> 26, y -> 15]]

#% text
# An integer with seven primes up to it, and a solution of two congruences:

#% cell
find_instance(prime_pi(n) == 7, n, integers)
#% output
#= [[n -> 17]]

#% cell
find_instance([mod(x, 3) == 1, mod(x, 5) == 2], x, integers)
#% output
#= [[x -> 7]]

#% text
# Seven squares, all of them positive:

#% cell
find_instance(x^2 + y^2 + z^2 + t^2 + u^2 + v^2 + w^2 == 1234567890987654321, [x, y, z, t, u, v, w], positive_integers)
#% output
#= [[x -> 1111111106, y -> 33333, z -> 148, t -> 17, u -> 1, v -> 1, w -> 1]]

#% text
# Primes and twin primes:

#% cell
find_instance(p > 10^10, p, primes)
#% output
#= [[p -> 10000000019]]

#% cell
rewrite(p, find_instance(element(p, primes) and element(p + 2, primes), p, 5))
#% output
#= [3, 5, 11, 17, 29]

#% text
# Several points of a periodic equation and of a finite set:

#% cell
find_instance(1 + cos(x) == 3/2, x, 3)
#% output
#= [[x -> (-1/3)*pi], [x -> (1/3)*pi], [x -> (5/3)*pi]]

#% cell
find_instance(x^2 + y^2 == 1 and x == 2*y + 1, [x, y], 5)
#% output
#= [[x -> -3/5, y -> -4/5], [x -> 1, y -> 0]]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `reduce` gives all the solutions:

#% cell
reduce(x^2 < 4 and x > 1, x)
#% output
#= 1 < x and x < 2

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `reduce`, `solve`
